meta {
  name: Update Lead (Invalid)
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads/{{leadId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "status": "Warm Prospect",
    "temperature": "Unknown"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 400 Bad Request", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(400);
  });

  test("reports a validation failure", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().message).to.equal("Validation failed");
  });

  test("names both offending fields", function () {
    const errors = JSON.stringify(res.getBody().errors);
    expect(errors).to.contain("status");
    expect(errors).to.contain("temperature");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /leads/:id — rejected values (PAC-38)

  Pins the write vocabulary. This request sends two values that read paths
  tolerate but writes must not accept.

  - **Module:** `leads`
  - **Permission:** `leads:write`

  ## What is being asserted

  **`status: "Warm Prospect"`** — an arbitrary label. `normalizeLeadStatus` is
  deliberately lenient on *read* (it passes unrecognized values through so a
  migrated record still renders as itself), but the write side only accepts
  canonical `LEAD_STATUSES` labels. That asymmetry is what makes the migrated
  mix converge over time rather than grow: every edit lands a canonical value.
  Raw SmartSuite codes (`arW7O`, `phjnb`, `jp76g`, `hfwda`) are rejected here
  for the same reason.

  **`temperature: "Unknown"`** — a valid `LEAD_TEMPERATURES` member and a
  perfectly good value to *receive*, but not one a producer may *choose*. It is
  the display state of a lead nobody has assessed. The DTO enumerates
  `LEAD_TEMPERATURE_OPTIONS` (`Hot`, `Warm`, `Cold`), which excludes it.

  An empty body `{}` is also a `400` — the schema requires at least one field,
  which is what makes this a patch rather than a no-op.

  ## Response `400`
  ```json
  {
    "message": "Validation failed",
    "errors": {
      "formErrors": [],
      "fieldErrors": {
        "status": ["Invalid enum value. ..."],
        "temperature": ["Invalid enum value. ..."]
      }
    }
  }
  ```

  Shape comes from `ZodValidationPipe` (`common/pipes/zod-validation.pipe.ts`),
  which flattens the zod error.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — the case under test.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `leads` module disabled, or missing `leads:write`.
  - `404 Not Found` — validation runs *after* the scope clamp, so an unowned
    lead is a 404 even with an invalid body.
}
